    <text xml:lang="ta" type="edition">
      <body>
        <div rend="parallel">
            <p xml:id="Aink442-kilavi">vēntaṟku uṟṟuḻip pirinta talaimakaṉ viṉaimuṭiyāmaiyiṉ pācaraikkaṇ iruntu paruva varaviṉkaṇ colliyatu.</p>
            <p xml:lang="en">The hero who had separated for a royal emergency, staying in the warcamp as [his] work was incomplete, when the season [of his intended return] came, spoke [this].</p>
              </div>
        <div rend="parallel">
          <lg xml:id="Aink442">        
            <l>peruñciṉa vēnta ṉaruntoḻi ṟaṇiyiṉ</l>
            <l>viruntunaṉi peṟutalu muriyaṇ mātō</l>
            <l>viruṇṭutōṉṟu vicumpi ṉuyarnilai yulakat</l>
            <l>taruntati yaṉaiya kaṟpiṟ</l>
            <l>kurumpai maṇippūṭ putalvaṉ ṟāyē.</l>
        </lg>  
 <lg xml:lang="en">                    
<l>If the king of great anger's difficult taksk is appeased,</l>
<l>will she not also be entitled to receive many guests --</l>
<l>the mother of the boy with ornaments with gems like young palm nuts<note xml:lang="en" place="foot">Presumably this is comparison of shape or size, rather then colour or another quality, but it is not clear to what type of ornament this comparison is made. The collocation <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">kurumpai maṇi pūṇ</foreign> is found in close proximity to <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">kiṇkiṇi</foreign> in NA269.1. If understood as a qualification the ornament, then this poem may also refer to a 'tinkling anklet'. In that poem, Wilden takes the phrase to be headed by <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">pūṇ</foreign> ('ornament') and translates it as a discrete ornament 'with unripe coconut-kernels for gems'.</note></l>
<l>of a virtue like that of Arundhati<note place="foot" xml:lang="en">
  <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">Aruntati</foreign> (Skt. <foreign xml:lang="sa-Latn">Arundhatī</foreign>) is the
  star Alcor in Ursa Major, known in Tamil also as <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">vaṭamīṉ</foreign> and
  <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">ciṟumīṉ</foreign>. This figure is invoked as a standard of <foreign xml:lang="ta-Latn">kaṟpu</foreign> on occasion in the Caṅkam corpus. See, for example, PN 122.8.</note></l>
<l>in the world of high station in the sky that appears dark.</l>
</lg>                      
        </div>
      </body>
    </text>
